    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let's get back to the math.\n\nIn the Fourier transform, the frequency resolution is determined by the sampling rate and the number of samples. Think of it like trying to distinguish between different types of goblin voices in a crowded tavern - the more samples you have, the more distinct voices you can hear.\n\nWhen it comes to identifying a burst of high-frequency jitter, the Fourier transform uses the concept of spectral power density. This is a measure of the energy or power contained in a particular frequency band. The transform calculates the power density by taking the absolute value of the Fourier coefficients and summing them up.\n\nTo differentiate between a burst of high-frequency jitter and a legitimate high-frequency component of the signal, the transform uses a technique called \"windowing.\" This involves applying a window function to the signal before taking the Fourier transform. The window function is like a goblin's cloak - it helps to smooth out the signal and reduce the impact of noise.\n\nThe window function can be thought of as a \"goblin-deterrent\" that helps to distinguish between signal and noise. By applying a window function, the transform can reduce the impact of noise and highlight the underlying signal.\n\nIn terms of",
